    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Puts Snow Tracks on Five SUVs and Trucks, Unloads Them at SEMA 2026 as the Snowhunter Bunch\nNostalgic About 1990s Chevrolet and Ford Models? Here's a Fox Body and Suburban Digital Ointment\n9 photos\nPhoto: the_kyza / Instagram\nKhyzyl Saleem, the virtual artist better known as \" the_kyza \" on social media, is thinking that now is maybe the perfect time to play with old things. After all, the member of the imaginative guild of digital car content creators hadn’t been dwelling across the parallel universes of vehicular CGI lately.\nInstead, the pixel master embarked on a wacky adventure after he joined the 2K Tour, an adventure involving ten teams that get $2k each to buy and modify a car – then drive it across 1,500 miles of “challenges and chaos.” One of the competitors managed to acquire a “ridiculous” Chevrolet Suburban for $2k, which they then drove across California & back, and that wasn’t all that happened, since it was driven as if it belonged on the track.\n“Did it belong on a track? No, absolutely not, but it sure inspired this thought of ‘what if’ it did!” So, the author set out to create ‘Big Red,’ a crimson Chevy ‘Burb that gives cheers to Scotty – aka Montgomery Scott, the fictional character in the science fiction franchise Star Trek that acts as Chief Engineer of the Enterprise ship from the original series and other entries of the franchise.\nThe CGI expert basically took an eighth-generation GMT400 Suburban and made it a slammed widebody track machine via a smooth aerodynamic body kit that includes a full widebody treatment along with a massive front spoiler and a large rear roof wing, among others. There are also bronze Rotiform six-spoke alloy wheels, massive track tires, a second wing for the dual rear doors, plus other ‘things.’\nThose ‘other things’ include the new suspension setup, a set of bucket seats up front, and a complete roll cage inside, including in the massive trunk. What do you think? Yay or nay? By the way, that’s not the only suggestion for a bonkers 1990s-style transformation, as an earlier project concerns a yellow Fox Body Ford Mustang with an attitude, riding on “thick” tires and BBS E88s with a set of cool side exhausts and a full aerodynamic widebody kit as if it’s about to break a new quarter-mile dragstrip class record or something…\nOf course, the current Chevrolet Suburban and Ford Mustang are a long way away from these 1990s-style builds. The General Motors SUV remains as hulking as ever at the top of the crossover and SUV roster of its Chevrolet subsidiary, starting from $63,700 and looking like it will never see the inside of a track unless it’s towing a dedicated ride.\nThe iconic Ford Mustang, meanwhile, is more and more a traditional sports car than a classic pony or muscle car, featuring the base EcoBoost turbo four-pot as an accessible option, but also the 5.0-liter Coyote V8 along with the 5.2-liter supercharged V8 on the Dark Horse SC and the ultra-expensive GTD. View this post on Instagram A post shared by Khyzyl Saleem (@the_kyza) View this post on Instagram A post shared by Khyzyl Saleem (@the_kyza)\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nChevrolet Suburban ford mustang fox body Chevrolet Suburban fox body CGI transformation rendering thekyza\nAbout the author: Aurel Niculescu\nAurel has aimed high all his life (literally, at 16 he was flying gliders all by himself) so in 2006 he switched careers and got hired as a writer at his favorite magazine. Since then, his work has been published both by print and online outlets, most recently right here, on autoevolution. Full profile\nUP NEXT Peanut Butter Is a Bamboo-Filled Camper Van With a Cozy Interior Built for Off-Grid Adventures",
